## Last text from previous session

ignal is needed, and the HB record is fully packed (prov@424, mepoch@456, feat@500 → 512), so c2 means carving the evict ring again and a proto-gen bump. Consulting before building that.
While that runs, verifying 0.19.5 (with the new fault disarmed by default) doesn't regress the board.
A thorough ruling that specifies a new on-disk protocol object. Recording it before anything else.
Board green on 0.19.5. The ruling's preferred design needs a new per-node on-disk region — let me check whether the envelope has reserved space, since that determines whether this needs an mkfs layout change.
Reached the relay boundary. One unblocking fact to record: mkfs packs the envelope with only 4K alignment between regions (`journal_offset = MXFS_SUPER_SIZE`, then `disklock_offset`, then `xfs_data_offset`) — there is **no reserved slack**, so the ruling's preferred dedicated progress table needs an mkfs layout change. Recording that before I stop.
**Criteria not met — MXFS is not production ready.** 51 defects remain open (37 critical). I did not write to the marker.

## What changed this session: 0.17.4 → 0.19.5, one critical closed

**`D-RELOG-BEHIND-DISK-OBLIGATION-DEADLOCK-WEDGE-380` — CLOSED, FIXED AND VERIFIED.** Two prior sessions had failed to reproduce it. The chain turned out to be generic, not specific to the fence the entry was named for: eleven `xfs_iflush` "safe skip" fences return `error = 0` without stamping `flush_seq`, so the publication obligation can never close; the release drain then re-logs, and `xfs_trans_log_inode` **incremented the very counter it was waiting on** (measured `pend=730 dur=6 flush=6`). Recovery depended on a local reader coincidentally arriving inside the 60 s bound, because `i_dlm_stale` had *no release-path consumer at all*.

Three fixes: one chokepoint for "this flush was abandoned", a release-side consumer that drives the reload the fence asked for (bounded, fail-closed, dropped changes still reported via P177), and suppression of both counter runaways under the existing `i_mxfs_pipe_relog` marker.

## The method that actually unlocked it

Not more code reading — **two test levers**. `mxfs.iflush_fence_fault_ino` turned a luck-dependent wedge into a 90 s reproduction; `mxfs.reldefer_noprogress_ms` shrank the *observation window* so real, unmodified fences became visible. That produced verification against both causes:

- **Injected**, paired A/B on one build: lever on → NO_WEDGE, mount UP; off → 689 fence hits, WEDGE, mount DOWN.
- **Natural**, no injection: pre-fix → `P228=10`, wedge, `ag_strand_repair` FAIL at 240/240; fixed → `P228=0`, no wedge, PASS at 81 s.

Full board at shipping defaults green on six successive builds.

## Three things I had to retract on my own evidence

I fixed a genuine contract violation (the P32E fence compared raw where the header mandates the predicate) and then **disproved it as the cause** — `RAWDIVERGE=0` over 14 firings. I recorded a "deterministic reproducer" after one success and the next two runs refuted it. And I wrote "one wedge took all 32 mounts down" into the ledger, then could not reproduce it in two further attempts; that entry now carries a 3-run scoreboard instead of the claim.

## Also advanced: `#474` hole (c), now reproducible for the first time

Built `mxfs.hold_grant_fault_ino` to produce the shape nothing in the tree could — a holder mounted and beating but refusing to release. Result: `P-WAIT-EXTEND ... el_ms=120159` and a **129.6 s acquire**, extended purely on heartbeat evidence. That confirmed hole (c) and, importantly, **reprioritized the fix**: the holder's filesystem was fully serviceable, so c1 cannot catch it and c2 is load-bearing. A RULE-5 ruling now specifies the design, and I established the gating blocker — mkfs packs the envelope with no reserved slack, so the preferred progress table needs a layout change, while the HB alternative sits inside a union that vanishes during recovery.

Rig is clean: 32/32 mounted, knobs at defaults.

